{
  "id": "ss.pickup.record",
  "name": "Field record",
  "description": "A page of a predecessor's log, lying where the swarm dropped it. Paper is the one thing on the field with no smell, so the hive carries it about and never eats it — which is why it survives, and why it turns up scattered rather than filed. Drawn as a page that has been through that: torn off along the top (the teeth on the outline are the tear), a nick out of one edge, folded once across the middle and opened again (the crease and the lit ridge under it), a crumple running in from the tear, and the bottom-right corner curled up off the ground and folded back over the page — the dark sliver between flap and cut is the ground showing under a corner that is not pressed flat. Bone-pale, lit along one side and in shade toward the curl, so it reads as a sheet with a bow in it rather than a rectangle. The writing is rows of hand rather than rules: a darker line at the head where the date goes, segments that drift a little off level, the last row short, and nothing running under the curl. The ground has marked it: a ring where something wet stood, a smear of soil at the foot, grit, and a faint trace of the hive that carried it. The halo is the brood cell's trick, paler: a thing meant to be found gets a little light of its own. The whole sheet lies eight degrees askew, because a dropped page never lands square.",
  "tags": ["ss-pickup"],
  "size": [32, 36],
